WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:06.080
For 400 million years sharks and other fish have&nbsp;
been living in our oceans. Us Humans have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:13.440
around for just about 300,000 years.
 
Plastic came along only very recently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.800
but if we were to continue to pollute&nbsp;
the oceans at the current pace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.680
it will take less than 30 years before there&nbsp;
is more plastic in the waters as there is fish.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.880
To put this into perspective:
Imagine sharks have been on this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:32.240
planet for one whole year. Just within&nbsp;
the last 15 seconds before midnight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:37.440
on December 31st, we would've managed&nbsp;
to pollute their natural habitat.

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:43.840
To sharks this is a tragedy&nbsp;
— a tragedy of the commons.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:52.480
The tragedy of the commons is a situation in which&nbsp;
individuals acting in their own self-interest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.000
behave contrary to the common&nbsp;
good of all others by depleting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.960
or spoiling the shared resource&nbsp;
through their collective action.
 &nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:08.240
The theory originated in an essay written in 1833&nbsp;
by the British economist William Forster Lloyd.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:14.400
Lloyd argued that if a herder led a large herd&nbsp;
of cattle onto a plot of common land to graze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.600
also known as commons, overgrazing could happen.

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:21.680
For each additional cow, the&nbsp;
owner of that cow could receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:26.320
additional benefits, while all other&nbsp;
herders share the resulting damage.

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.040
There are three possible solutions&nbsp;
that could help with this.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:36.960
Social norms and a common understanding of what's&nbsp;
right and what's wrong is probably our best bet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:42.320
The Swiss Alps region for example has been run&nbsp;
by a collective of farmers for hundreds of years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.280
As it is in the interest of all the farmers to&nbsp;
keep the commons functioning, complex social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:53.360
norms emerged, and over time became traditions.&nbsp;
But when people don’t share the same values,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.960
or new people arrive, such social contracts&nbsp;
often fall apart and other solutions can help.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:06.960
Privatization. Turning common property into a&nbsp;
private one can give the owner an incentive to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:11.360
ensure its sustainability. If that happens&nbsp;
it’s important that the party who now owns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:17.280
that common — or rights of access — pays the&nbsp;
full price for its exploitation. Privatization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:22.400
works well for things that can be fenced off,&nbsp;
such as land, but less so for water or air.&nbsp;

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.360
If someone creates negative externalities&nbsp;
on private land but doesn’t pay the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.960
price of such externality, we need&nbsp;
to turn to the third solution.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:38.320
State Regulation, such as farming permits,&nbsp;
can limit the amount of commons available to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:43.840
everyone and thereby protect them. Fishing&nbsp;
quotas can ensure that those that take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:49.200
also pay the bill. Taxes can create financial&nbsp;
incentives by increasing the costs for creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:54.640
negative externalities, such as polluting the air.&nbsp;
Regulation can make exploitation not only more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:03:00.000
costly, tax incomes can also be used to invest&nbsp;
in initiatives that help reverse the damage done.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:05.680
For complex problems, such&nbsp;
as the plastic in our oceans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.400
we probably need a combination of all three.

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:12.400
We can help create awareness, so we&nbsp;
all feel morally obliged to reduce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:17.280
our personal use of non recyclable plastics.
We could ask large producers of single use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.040
plastics to take full responsibility&nbsp;
for the mess their products create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:26.000
by dedicating resources to help clean up.
Countries could commit to local and international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:31.840
agreements that regulate the production of&nbsp;
single use plastic and other pollutants.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:37.600
The grazing rules which were established in&nbsp;
Switzerland in 1517 are still enforced today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.200
They state that "no one is permitted to send more&nbsp;
cows to the Alps than he can feed in winter".&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.440
Applying the same rules to plastics, would&nbsp;
mean no state ought to produce more plastic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:51.840
than they can recycle within the confines&nbsp;
of their own borders. What do you think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.040
would a similar solution work for the oceans?&nbsp;
And if not, what else can we do to protect the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:13.840
commons from the tragedy that's unfolding?&nbsp;
